<Post title="Probing the silence" part={2} date="2026-09-17" series="rshunterbtt">
	<p class="tagline">
		The decompiled app told me what the protocol should do. Getting the hardware to agree
		meant building a probe, deploying it to the same underpowered boards the real bridge
		runs on, and recording what actually came back — including a few answers that were not
		the ones I expected.
	</p>

	<h2><span class="anchor-hash">#</span>the probe</h2>
	<p>
		Rather than reason from a spec that doesn't exist, I wrote a throwaway BLE probe: a
		small Rust binary using the same BLE library as the real bridge, cross-compiled
		statically for the target (ARMv6, musl), copied onto the Pi hosts, and run next to a
		full HCI capture. The probe connects, prints the raw status characteristic, subscribes
		to notifications and prints them, and can be told to sit idle, reconnect, or request
		connection parameters. Every run pairs the application-level view with the controller's
		own event stream, so there is no guessing about who did what.
	</p>
	<p>
		The question it existed to answer was narrow and practical: how do I make a zone query
		come back fast enough, and reliably enough, that the voice assistant stops giving up
		and marking the zone offline?
	</p>

	<h2><span class="anchor-hash">#</span>first result: the ground truth, and the bug confirmed</h2>
	<p>
		Connecting to two of the three timers returned byte-identical status payloads:
	</p>
	<pre><code>0000000000000000000001018080</code></pre>
	<p>
		Read through the layout from part 1: bytes <strong>10 and 11</strong> are <code>0x01</code> — both zones reporting active — while bytes <strong>4 and 8</strong>,
		the manual-enable flags the old bridge was reading, are <code>0x00</code>. Same payload,
		opposite conclusions depending on which byte you trust. That is the byte-offset bug,
		sitting in the first capture, in black and white.
	</p>
	<p>
		It also produced an immediate puzzle. Both zones "active" on an idle timer? Either the
		device's state byte means something subtler than "watering", or the timers were mid-run.
		That thread gets pulled properly in part 4 — and the answer is that the status byte was
		never a boolean.
	</p>

	<h2><span class="anchor-hash">#</span>the host controller is a character in this story</h2>
	<p>
		The boards are Raspberry Pi Zero W units with the BCM43430A1 combo chip, and their
		Bluetooth controllers are not reliable under load. Early captures showed the kernel
		logging <code>hci0: Opcode 0x2005 failed: -16</code> (EBUSY) right alongside connection
		failures. I initially read <code>0x2005</code> as "create connection". It isn't —
		<code>0x2005</code> is <em>LE Set Random Address</em>; create-connection is <code>0x200d</code>. The host address on these boards changes between connections, and
		setting it occasionally collides with whatever else the controller is doing.
	</p>
	<p>
		The decisive observation was simpler and more annoying: one unit stopped connecting <em>even with fresh batteries</em>, and stayed that way across service restarts. A full
		reboot of the Pi fixed it instantly. That is the signature of a wedged host controller,
		not a dead device — and it is the reason the final design includes recovery actions that
		reach all the way up to rebooting the host.
	</p>

	<h2><span class="anchor-hash">#</span>the baseline, and a misleading error</h2>
	<p>An early HCI trace, straight from the controller:</p>
	<pre><code>LE Create Connection   min 30ms  max 60ms  latency 0  supervision 3000ms
LE Connection Complete Success, interval 60ms, latency 0, supervision 3000ms
LE Read Remote Used Features -> Connection Failed to be Established (0x3e)
HCI Disconnect (Reason: Remote User Terminated Connection 0x13)</code></pre>
	<p>
		Two lessons. First, the default connection interval is <strong>60 ms</strong> — which is
		about to become the whole story in part 5. Second, the disconnect reason reads like the
		device hung up. It doesn't. The host issued that disconnect itself, after the device
		stopped answering a feature read. The stack's "terminated by local" language tells you
		which side of <em>the host's own stack</em> generated the teardown, not which physical
		side walked away. Read literally, it sends you hunting for device-side sleep behaviour
		that isn't there.
	</p>

	<h2><span class="anchor-hash">#</span>four quick tests that decided the architecture</h2>
	<p>
		With the basics understood, four questions had to be answered before committing to a
		rewrite. Each got a small experiment, and each one changed the plan.
	</p>

	<h3>1. Will the device hold an idle connection? <span class="badge">PASS</span></h3>
	<p>
		Connect, subscribe, then do nothing at all. If the timer dropped idle links, the whole
		persistent-connection idea was dead. It held for <strong>~38.5 minutes</strong> with no
		disconnect event before I cut the test short to save time. The hypothesis that the device
		immediately drops idle connections was dead instead.
	</p>

	<h3>2. Does it push state changes on its own? <span class="badge">PASS</span></h3>
	<p>
		Still connected, still zero requests being sent — then a physical button press on the
		timer. The device pushed notifications, unprompted:
	</p>
	<pre><code>00:34:20  Notification: 0000000000010000000009010000   <- press (zone started)
00:34:26  Notification: 0000000000000000000001010000   <- press (zone stopped)
00:34:26  Notification: 0000000000000000000001018000   <- follow-up state</code></pre>
	<p>
		This is the single most useful fact about the hardware. The device narrates its own state
		changes. A bridge that subscribes can know what the timer is doing — including runs it
		didn't start — without polling. (The middle digit of that first payload is load-bearing,
		and part 4 explains why.)
	</p>

	<h3>3. Will it accept a slower connection interval? <span class="badge">PASS</span></h3>
	<p>
		Connection interval is the lever for battery: fewer connection events per second means
		less radio time, but each round-trip waits longer. Using the controller's connection
		update mechanism directly, with a capture running to see what the device actually agreed
		to:
	</p>
	<table>
		<thead>
			<tr><th>requested interval</th><th>result</th></tr>
		</thead>
		<tbody>
			<tr><td>1000 ms</td><td>accepted</td></tr>
			<tr><td>2000 ms</td><td>accepted (first attempt failed on an out-of-range supervision timeout)</td></tr>
			<tr><td>4000 ms</td><td><strong>accepted — the BLE maximum</strong></td></tr>
		</tbody>
	</table>
	<p>
		Four seconds is the spec ceiling for a connection interval, so the device is willing to
		go all the way; that is a 66× reduction in connection events versus the 60 ms default.
		The first attempt at this test produced a wrong conclusion — the tool reported a timeout
		and I wrote "device rejects updates" — before the capture showed the device had simply
		responded <em>slowly</em>, around ten seconds later. The device is not being difficult on
		purpose; it is just not in a hurry.
	</p>

	<h3>4. Does reconnecting restore everything? <span class="badge">PASS, with a caveat</span></h3>
	<p>
		Force a disconnect, reconnect, and re-subscribe. A button press then arrived correctly.
		But the subscription itself <strong>does not survive a drop</strong> — after any
		disconnect, the CCCD write must be redone from scratch. Recovery is a full re-run of
		connection setup, not a resume.
	</p>
	<h2><span class="anchor-hash">#</span>what the battery data actually measures</h2>
	<p>
		The interval only matters because of battery, so I went looking for the real numbers:
		about 120,000 battery readings, two and a half years, resampled to daily medians to
		strip the noise, with cycle boundaries detected from the jumps back to 100% and
		corrected for the weeks when a device sat dead before anyone swapped its cells.
	</p>
	<ul>
		<li>Corrected average life: <strong>~9.5–10.2 months</strong> on two AA cells.</li>
		<li>Typical cycle: 298–337 days; the shortest observed was about 216 days.</li>
		<li>Several cycles ended a week or three before the batteries were actually replaced.</li>
	</ul>
	<p>
		There is an important caveat underneath that average. The logger behind those numbers
		was, in practice, <em>disconnected most of the time</em> — it connected briefly, polled,
		and dropped. So every one of those ten months measures the <em>connect-briefly</em> pattern, not a persistent connection. The battery cost of keeping a link open at any
		interval is still a projection; the only way to replace it with a fact is to deploy the
		thing and watch it for a few weeks.
	</p>

	<h2><span class="anchor-hash">#</span>the verdict</h2>
	<p>
		Persistent connection plus notifications is technically sound: the device holds the
		link, narrates its own state, accepts the slowest legal interval, and recovers from a
		drop with a full re-setup. Every one of those was a gate, and every gate passed — which
		meant the flaky production bridge could be rebuilt around the opposite architecture.
	</p>
	<p>
		What I did <em>not</em> have was a measurement for the thing the rewrite was for. That
		uncertainty comes along for the ride.
	</p>

	<p>
		Next: the rewrite itself — one connection held open, authenticated, notification-driven,
		and supervised by a ladder of increasingly violent recovery actions.
	</p>
</Post>
